<TopicPage slug="subjects/aviation-maintenance/foundational/amt-general">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Aviation maintenance is mechanical craft under a legal microscope. When an aircraft
    maintenance technician (AMT) signs a record, that signature carries federal legal weight:
    it certifies that specified work was performed to approved standards and that the
    aircraft is fit to fly. That context changes what "fundamentals" means. The General
    curriculum — the first certificate block every AMT studies, before the Airframe and
    Powerplant specialties — is not a tour of handy facts; it is the shared toolkit of maths,
    physics, drawings and regulations that every later task assumes, plus the professional
    habit that defines the trade: every task is measured, every measurement recorded, and
    every record signed with legal weight.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why so much rigour? Because in aviation, errors fly. A loose fitting, an untorqued bolt
    or an out-of-limits centre of gravity does not sit in a closet waiting to be found — it
    is tested in turbulence, at altitude, at landing. Aviation's remarkable safety record is
    the product of a system built on that fact: approved data, exact specifications,
    traceable parts, duplicate inspections and a documentation culture where "if it is not
    documented, it did not happen" is not a slogan but a legal reality.
  </p>
  <p>
    This page covers the General curriculum's three pillars — aircraft drawings and the maths
    that backs them, the physics a mechanic uses daily, and the regulatory framework that
    authorises the wrench — then deepens each. The running example is weight and balance,
    because it uses all three at once: drawings give the dimensions, physics gives the
    moments, and regulations give the limits the arithmetic must land inside.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="aircraft as system, maths, drawings, regulations, inspection" widget="equationStep" config={generalCfg} />

  <h2>Aircraft drawings and maths</h2>
  <p>
    Drawings are the instructions, and reading them is a tested skill. <strong>Engineering
    drawings</strong> use orthographic projection — several flat views (front, top, side)
    of one object, each view aligned with the others — plus dimensions, tolerances and title
    blocks that state the part number, revision and material. A revision letter matters more
    than it looks: using superseded drawings is using wrong data, which in aviation is not
    merely a mistake but a regulatory event. <strong>Schematics</strong> are the system
    cousins of drawings: wiring diagrams show electrical logic (symbols and wire numbers, not
    physical routing), hydraulic and fuel system schematics show flow paths, and the
    mechanic's diagnostic work usually begins by tracing a schematic to form a hypothesis
    before touching hardware.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>maths</strong> that backs the drawings is mostly applied arithmetic with
    careful units. Aircraft maintenance lives across two measurement systems — FAA materials
    in US customary units (inches, pounds), most of the world in metric — so unit conversion
    is a survival skill (2.54 cm per inch, 2.2 lb per kg approximately, litres to gallons).
    Precision matters at the tool level too: a mechanic reads micrometers and calipers to
    thousandths, computes tolerances and clearances, works with ratios and percentages
    (fuel mixtures, gear ratios), and calculates areas and volumes for everything from
    fabric coverage to cylinder displacement. The habit drilled hardest: carry the units
    through every step, because a unit error in aviation arithmetic is a 90-kg fuel load
    becoming 90 litres — and only one of those is survivable arithmetic.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Weight and balance</strong> is the signature calculation, done before every
    flight and formally whenever equipment changes. The physics is a lever balance: every
    item's weight times its distance from a reference point (the <em>datum</em>) is a
    <em>moment</em>, and the aircraft's centre of gravity is total moment divided by total
    weight. Run the widget's numbers on a light aircraft (650 kg empty): 120 L of fuel
    weighs 120 × 0.72 ≈ 86 kg, so with an 80 kg pilot and 20 kg of baggage the ramp weight
    is about 836 kg — comfortably under a 1,050 kg limit. But the same arithmetic on the
    <em>arms</em> must also land the CG inside its certified window; fuel, pilot and baggage
    all compete for that window, and loading outside it is an unairworthy aircraft no matter
    how healthy its engine.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="a light aircraft's weight-and-balance check" widget="sliderLab" config={wbCfg} />

  <h2>Physics for aviation maintenance</h2>
  <p>
    The FAA General Handbook opens with physics because the hangar uses it daily. <strong>Simple
    machines</strong> underlie every tool: the torque wrench is a lever (torque = force ×
    length — why a longer wrench needs less hand force for the same bolt tension, and why
    using a cheater bar on a torque wrench silently destroys its calibration), pulleys and
    gears set cable tensions and magneto timing. <strong>Fluid mechanics</strong> explains
    both the aircraft and the shop: Pascal's principle — pressure applied to a confined fluid
    transmits equally in all directions — is why a small brake pedal force becomes a large
    caliper force, why hydraulic jacks lift aircraft, and why a contaminated hydraulic system
    is a grounding event. Bernoulli and pressure differentials explain pitot-static systems,
    fuel flow and the venting that keeps tanks from collapsing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Heat and matter</strong> complete the daily set: thermal expansion (why
    clearances are specified at temperature, why engines are warmed before full power —
    metals grow measurably with heat), heat transfer by conduction and convection (why oil
    coolers exist, why bimetallic strips drive cylinder-head temperature gauges), and the
    behaviour of gases under compression (why compressed-air systems need dryers, why
    pressure vessels are certified). Materials science enters through the same door:
    aluminium alloys' fatigue behaviour is why hard landings trigger inspections, and
    understanding stress, strain and why bolts stretch into their torque range is why the
    trade torques rather than "tightens".
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> aircraft mechanics need the physics to build aircraft. The
    physics is for <em>deciding</em>: whether a measured wear limit is acceptable, why a
    hydraulic system behaves this way when the pressure gauge says that, whether a cable
    tension reading is plausible. The General physics exists to turn wrench-pullers into
    diagnosticians.
  </p>

  <h2>Regulations, privileges and forms</h2>
  <p>
    The regulatory layer is what makes an AMT different from a good mechanic. Under United
    States rules, <strong>FAA Part 65</strong> defines the mechanic certificate: Airframe
    and/or Powerplant ratings earned through an FAA-approved programme (Part 147 schools)
    or experience, plus written, oral and practical examinations. The certificate defines
    <em>privileges and limits</em>: a certificated mechanic may perform maintenance and
    approve an aircraft for return to service — with defined exceptions (major repairs and
    major alterations need additional authorisation; some work is reserved for repair
    stations; inspections require an Inspection Authorisation for annuals).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>FAA Part 43</strong> defines what "maintenance" legally means — inspection,
    overhaul, repair, preservation, and the replacement of parts — and, crucially, the
    <em>performance rules</em>: use of methods and practices acceptable to the Administrator
    (the manufacturer's maintenance manual or accepted references such as AC 43.13-1B),
    proper tools, calibrated instruments where required, and the record entries that make
    the work legally exist. The paperwork system has named artefacts: <strong>airworthiness
    directives</strong> (ADs) are legally mandatory corrections issued when the FAA finds a
    safety problem — an uncomplied AD grounds the aircraft; <strong>Form 337</strong>
    documents major repairs and alterations; and the aircraft's <strong>logbooks</strong>
    carry every maintenance entry with date, description, reference data and signature.
    Falsifying maintenance records is a federal offence — the paper trail is the safety
    system.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the regulations are bureaucracy layered on the "real" work.
    In aviation they are the work's authorisation layer: the same bolt torqued with approved
    data, acceptable tools and a proper entry is maintenance; without the entry it is an
    unairworthy aircraft. The signature is the product.
  </p>

  <h2>Tool control and calibration culture</h2>
  <p>
    The General curriculum's habits show in the tool box. <strong>Torque wrenches</strong>
    are precision instruments: used smoothly to a stop (never with a jerking pull), stored
    unwound to protect the spring, and sent for <strong>calibration</strong> on schedule —
    with the calibration certificate traceable to national standards, because an untraceable
    torque value is an unknown torque value. <strong>Tool control</strong> programmes —
    shadowed tool boxes where every tool has an outlined home, inventories checked at shift
    end — exist because a forgotten wrench in an engine bay is a foreign object damage (FOD)
    event waiting for the next flight, and FOD is the maintenance world's classic preventable
    catastrophe. <strong>Measuring instruments</strong> (micrometers, dial indicators,
    cable tensiometers) live the same discipline: zero-checked before use, handled like
    instruments rather than tools, and calibrated against known references. The culture's
    summary is one line: in aviation, a tool's accuracy is a document, not an assumption —
    and the document chain from wrench to national standard is what makes "torque to 35 N·m"
    mean the same thing in every hangar on Earth.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The weight-and-balance arithmetic deserves its worked example.</strong> Suppose
    the light aircraft above has arms (distances aft of the datum) of 2.0 m for the empty
    airframe, 2.4 m for the pilot, 3.5 m for baggage and 2.6 m for the fuel tank. The moments
    are 650 × 2.0 = 1,300; 80 × 2.4 = 192; 20 × 3.5 = 70; and 86.4 × 2.6 ≈ 225 kg·m. Total
    moment ≈ 1,787 kg·m over 836 kg gives a CG near 2.14 m aft of datum — and the airframe's
    certified window (say 2.00–2.30 m) is the verdict. Notice what the arithmetic reveals:
    burning fuel moves the CG (the tank's arm no longer carries its moment), so the check is
    done for takeoff <em>and</em> landing weights; and a heavy pilot plus full fuel can pass
    the weight limit while failing the CG limit — two separate gates, both mandatory.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Human factors are part of the General curriculum</strong> because the system's
    honest failure mode is the technician's own state. The FAA teaches the "dirty dozen" —
    lack of communication, complacency, lack of knowledge, distraction, fatigue, stress and
    the rest — each with defences (checklists, team briefings, the "it's late, re-verify"
    rule). Maintenance-error investigations repeatedly find correct skills defeated by
    correctable conditions, which is why modern programmes treat human-factors training as
    certification content, not soft-skills decoration.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Inspection closes the loop</strong> and gives the trade its rhythm: annual
    inspections (required for most aircraft every 12 calendar months), 100-hour inspections
    for aircraft flying for hire, progressive inspection programmes, and the recurring AD
    compliance schedule — all instances of one principle: verification by someone other than
    the performer, against published standards, recorded permanently. The Airframe and
    Powerplant specialties that follow assume every habit General built: exact torque values,
    approved data, traceable parts, and a signature that means what it says. Aviation's
    safety record is thousands of tasks done to specification, documented, and inspected
    again by someone else — a system the General curriculum is the entry ticket to.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The publication hierarchy deserves its own map.</strong> Approved data flows to
    the wrench through a chain of documents, each with legal standing: the aircraft
    maintenance manual and component manuals (the manufacturer's methods); service
    bulletins (manufacturer-issued guidance, mandatory only when invoked by an AD);
    structural repair manuals for damage limits; and the FAA's advisory circulars
    (acceptable methods where the manufacturer is silent — AC 43.13-1B being the classic).
    Revisions are the lifeblood of the system: manuals are updated continuously, and the
    effective revision is the only one that counts — an out-of-date manual is legally not
    the aircraft's manual. The mechanic's daily literacy is checking effective pages and
    revision status before using a procedure, logging the reference data in the
    maintenance entry, and knowing that "the way the old-timer does it" has no standing
    against any of the above. Regulation, in other words, is not the opposite of craft
    knowledge here — it is the filing system that makes craft knowledge trustworthy at
    fleet scale.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> FAA Part 147 curriculum and FAA-H-8083-30A General Handbook; FAA Part 43/65; FAA AC 43.13-1B.</p>
</TopicPage>
